Cache Eviction Strategies

Why eviction?
	Cache memory is limited. When the cache is full and a new item needs to be stored, an eviction policy decides which existing item to remove.
	
1. LRU — Least Recently Used
	Evict the item that was used least recently.
	Idea: If something hasn't been used recently, it is less likely to be needed soon.
	
2. LFU — Least Frequently Used
	Evict the item accessed the fewest times.
	Idea: Keep the most popular data.
	
3. FIFO — First In, First Out
	Evict the item that entered the cache first.
	Accessing an item doesn't change its position.
	
4. Random Replacement (RR)
	Evict a random item.
	Use when: you want minimal complexity/overhead.
	
5. MRU — Most Recently Used
	Evict the item used most recently.
	Idea: The item you just used may be a one-time/temporary access and therefore less likely to be needed again soon.
	Use when: recent access actually predicts less future reuse.
	
6. TTL — Time To Live
	Remove an item after a fixed amount of time.
	TTL is particularly useful when data freshness matters.
	
7. Two-Tier Caching
	This is slightly different: instead of just deciding what to evict, you use two cache layers.
	L1: Local cache
		Very fast
		Lives inside application server
		Smaller
		Examples: HashMap, LRU cache
	L2: Remote cache
		Shared between application servers
		Larger
		Network access required
		Examples: Redis, Memcached
		
Quick comparison
	| Strategy     | Evicts                | Main idea              | Typical use                          |
	| ------------ | --------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
	| **LRU**      | Least recently used   | Recent = valuable      | General-purpose cache                |
	| **LFU**      | Least frequently used | Popular = valuable     | Hot/popular data                     |
	| **FIFO**     | Oldest item           | First in → first out   | Simple workloads                     |
	| **Random**   | Random item           | No tracking            | Very simple/unpredictable workloads  |
	| **MRU**      | Most recently used    | Recent = less valuable | Special workloads                    |
	| **TTL**      | Expired item          | Freshness matters      | Time-sensitive data                  |
	| **Two-Tier** | Depends on each layer | Local + shared cache   | High-performance distributed systems |

